Pipe-Lung

Creative Catalyst: The genre was born deep in the crust, where miners rhythmically struck ventilation shafts with pneumatic wrenches to pass the time.

Pipe-Lung is a claustrophobic yet strangely comforting collision of industrial percussion and soul-drenched groove. It discards the typical "bouncy" energy of liquid funk, replacing it with a dense, pressurized atmosphere that feels like oxygen being slowly withdrawn from a room. The Hammond organ provides a grit-caked, gospel-inflected warmth, while distorted 1980s rock guitars provide a jagged, metallic edge. It is music meant for listening in confined, dim spaces, sounding as if it is being played through a speaker encased in a damp, rusted iron box.

Genre DNA
*   Tempo & Pulse: 145–155 BPM; syncopated, sub-heavy rhythmic structures that mimic the stuttering repetition of heavy machinery.
*   Harmonic Palette: Bluesy pentatonics pushed through aggressive distortion; heavy use of "dirty" diminished chords to replicate the unease of being deep underground.
*   Production Signatures: 
       The Resonant Duct Mic Technique:* Placing microphones inside corrugated steel piping to capture a natural, metallic-filtered room decay.
       Voltage-Sag Compression:* Simulating 1980s power instability, where the drums "duck" or lose volume whenever a heavy, distorted guitar note is played.
       Humidity Saturation:* A proprietary tape-warbling effect that makes every track sound like it was recorded in a high-pressure, oxygen-rich environment.
*   Dynamics: A slow, creeping "pressure cooker" build where the background ambience thickens until the rhythm suddenly collapses into a sparse, clean organ solo.

Scene & Origin Lore
Pipe-Lung emerged from the "Shaft 9" miners in the Ural Mountains during the late 80s. When the main ventilation failed, the crew began drumming on the pipes to keep their blood moving and sanity intact. The fashion is strictly "utilitarian-gothic": oil-stained boiler suits accessorized with rusted copper jewelry and headlamps used as strobe lights. Their rituals include a communal "Listening Hour" at the end of shifts, where they sit in absolute darkness while a tape of their own rhythmic pipe-clanking plays, accompanied by a single, mournful Hammond organ melody.

Typical Instruments: 1982 Hammond B3 (run through a shattered Leslie cabinet), modified Fender Stratocaster with a high-gain "sand-blast" tone, pressurized ventilation pipes used as percussion, and 8-bit drum machines buried under heavy grit.

For Fans Of: Burial, King Gizzard & The Lizard Wizard (early stuff), John Carpenter, The Stooges.

